WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.518
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
you've built your machine

00:00:02.518 --> 00:00:04.010
learning models,
and now it's time

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:05.730
to take them to production.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.580
What do you need to do?

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.485
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.485 --> 00:00:12.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.970
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.560
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.740
I am Priyanka Vergadia,
and today, we're

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.100
going to talk about managing
production machine learning

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.240
pipelines.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.240
As you've seen through
[? years ?] of AI Adventures,

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.000
machine learning is
a lot of fun and is

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.860
much more than just a novelty.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.890
Machine learning models power so
many technologies in our lives,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.180
and there are plenty of new
and better models just waiting

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.440
to be applied to the real world.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.250
So how do you transition
from experimenting

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:52.430
with a model to actually
moving it to production?

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:56.180
Well, just as you need
a quality CI/CD pipeline

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.850
to maintain a robust
code base at scale,

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.860
you need a machine
learning pipeline

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.620
to help you monitor,
transform, evaluate,

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.660
and serve your
models in production.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.400
What are the different
steps involved

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.440
in a machine learning workflow?

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.680
Well, first, you ingest the
input data set and split it

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.320
for training,
evaluation, and testing.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:16.970
Then, validate it.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.670
Identify and remove
anomalies from that data,

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:22.760
or handle redundant
features and empty values.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.250
You might have to address
the detected issues,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.040
maybe finding more training
data for certain categories.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.090
Then, you do feature engineering
to transform the data

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.980
and enrich the
features to improve

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.890
your training, at which
point the model is

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.780
ready to train and tune.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.300
Once trained, evaluate
the model to make

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.170
sure it generalizes well
enough to use in production.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.000
When the model gets
the green light,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.800
you push it to production.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.390
This production
environment needs

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.410
to be powerful enough to support
the rate of inference required

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.040
by your application.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:01.390
Now, when does this ML
workflow become a pipeline?

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.530
Well, it will be automated,
and the automation

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.140
is necessary, especially
if we want to deploy

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.070
these models in production.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:11.550
So the question now
becomes, how do we

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.010
automate the machine
learning workflow tasks

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.190
to create an end-to-end
pipeline for deploying

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.110
our models into production?

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.440
Here is a very high level
architecture of how it works.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.770
TensorFlow is running on
TFX, which in turn is running

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.050
inside Kubeflow
pipelines, and that's

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.650
running on GKE, leveraging
the Google Cloud Services.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.710
You can create pipelines
with similar functionality

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:39.220
using both Kubeflow pipelines
and TensorFlow extended SDKs.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.490
We have an entire
series on Kubeflow,

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.030
which I have linked below.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.420
In this video, let's
focus on TensorFlow

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.150
extended, or TFX, which
is an open source,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.850
end-to-end platform
specifically designed

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.900
for scalable, high performance,
machine learning tasks.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.430
Here's how it works.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:00.810
A TFX pipeline is a sequence of
components implementing the ML

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.030
workflow tasks,
and they are built

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.820
using open source
TFX libraries, which

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.800
can also be used individually.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.710
This is sort of a canonical
TFX effect pipeline.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.920
You can think of it as
hello world pipeline.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.040
These orange components come
out of the box with TensorFlow

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.150
extended, but you can create
your own components as well.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.400
Once the model is
ready, you deploy it

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.070
into a serving cluster
in TensorFlow serving,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:30.000
TensorFlow.js, TensorFlow
Lite, TensorFlow Hub, or even

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.560
AI platform predictions.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.860
Now, what is a TFX component?

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.760
It is essentially a task that we
perform in our machine learning

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.540
pipeline.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.650
It has a config, and it takes
input from the metadata,

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.880
except for the very
first component

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.740
which is ingesting the data set
from the original data source.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:54.150
It takes those inputs using an
input channel, does its task,

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.580
and generates the
output artifact

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.910
and then uses the output
channel to put those back

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:03.210
into the metadata, and
that's how components

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.220
communicate with each other.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.070
The upstream components
will produce an artifact

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:10.530
that the downstream
component depends on,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.870
and the data flows
through the pipeline

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.870
by flowing through these
dependencies in the metadata.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.600
Now, what is metadata?

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.370
Well, it allows us to trace the
lineage of the artifacts that

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:28.650
are produced, for example, what
data is the model trained with?

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:32.970
Metadata can also be used to
compare previous training runs.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.850
It also allows
tracking and reuse

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.250
of previously
computed outputs which

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.620
saves quite a bit of time,
especially for larger data

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.200
sets and models.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.330
If the input to the
component has not changed,

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.120
we don't want to
rerun the component.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.750
We should just be able to pull
the last result from the cache

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.190
and reuse it.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.560
Now, let's go back to
our standard components

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.780
and understand them
a little bit more.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.130
First, off, ExampleGen helps us
ingest our data from CSV files,

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.320
BigQuery, Presto, et cetera.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.460
It splits the data and saves
it off as TF examples and TF

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.990
record files.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.230
StatisticsGen will make a
full pass over that data

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.540
and calculate
statistics about it.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.810
That's important to understand
our data, if it is new

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.150
and if it has changed over time.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.760
SchemaGen then
determines the schema

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:27.330
across our data, such as what
are the types of our features,

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.310
and for the
categorical variables,

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.350
what are the valid categories?

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.040
Example Validator takes both
of those results and looks

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:37.920
for problems, things
like the examples

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.770
that have the wrong type
in the particular feature

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:44.100
or a categorical value
that should not be there.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.380
Transform is where the
feature engineering is done.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.650
It will convert the
feature engineering

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:54.540
that we want to do with a graph
that becomes part of our model.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.150
Trainer takes that
graph and the model

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.460
that we have defined
using TensorFlow

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.650
and trains that model.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.050
Evaluator takes
that trained model

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:07.710
and does a deep analysis of
the performance of that model.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:11.010
It looks at not just the
entire data set but also

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:14.190
the individual slices
of a data to try

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:17.340
to understand how performance
varies across different parts

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.550
of our data.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.850
It can be used for
fairness analysis,

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:23.820
like seeing if your model
is performing differently

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.060
for different types
of model users.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.330
InfraValidator makes
sure that we can actually

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:33.750
deploy our model to our
serving infrastructure.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.540
If both Evaluator
and InfraValidator

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.720
agree that, yes, we
can deploy our model,

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:43.020
our Evaluator has
checked to make sure

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:46.050
that the model is actually
better than what we have

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:49.320
in production, then
the Pusher will push it

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.900
to one of our
deployment targets.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.020
So that was a quick overview
of TensorFlow Extended which

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.630
helps us engineer our
machine learning pipelines,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.710
so we can deploy our
models in production.

